#: ../../../building_robot.md:1
msgid "Building your own robot"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:3
msgid ""
"In this tutorial we will learn how to build our own robot in SDFormat. We "
"will build a simple two wheeled robot."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:5
msgid ""
"You can find the finished SDF file for the tutorial [here](https://github."
"com/gazebosim/docs/blob/master/rotary/tutorials/building_robot/"
"building_robot.sdf)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:7
msgid "What is SDF"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:9
msgid ""
"[SDFormat](http://sdformat.org/) (Simulation Description Format), sometimes "
"abbreviated as SDF, is an XML format that describes objects and environments "
"for robot simulators, visualization, and control."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:11
msgid "Building a world"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:13
msgid ""
"We will start by building a simple world and then build our robot in it. "
"Open a new file called `building_robot.sdf` and copy the following code to "
"it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:15
msgid ""
"<?xml version=\"1.0\" ?>\n"
"<sdf version=\"1.10\">\n"
"    <world name=\"car_world\">\n"
"        <physics name=\"1ms\" type=\"ignored\">\n"
"            <max_step_size>0.001</max_step_size>\n"
"            <real_time_factor>1.0</real_time_factor>\n"
"        </physics>\n"
"        <plugin\n"
"            filename=\"gz-sim-physics-system\"\n"
"            name=\"gz::sim::systems::Physics\">\n"
"        </plugin>\n"
"        <plugin\n"
"            filename=\"gz-sim-user-commands-system\"\n"
"            name=\"gz::sim::systems::UserCommands\">\n"
"        </plugin>\n"
"        <plugin\n"
"            filename=\"gz-sim-scene-broadcaster-system\"\n"
"            name=\"gz::sim::systems::SceneBroadcaster\">\n"
"        </plugin>\n"
"\n"
"        <light type=\"directional\" name=\"sun\">\n"
"            <cast_shadows>true</cast_shadows>\n"
"            <pose>0 0 10 0 0 0</pose>\n"
"            <diffuse>0.8 0.8 0.8 1</diffuse>\n"
"            <specular>0.2 0.2 0.2 1</specular>\n"
"            <attenuation>\n"
"                <range>1000</range>\n"
"                <constant>0.9</constant>\n"
"                <linear>0.01</linear>\n"
"                <quadratic>0.001</quadratic>\n"
"            </attenuation>\n"
"            <direction>-0.5 0.1 -0.9</direction>\n"
"        </light>\n"
"\n"
"        <model name=\"ground_plane\">\n"
"            <static>true</static>\n"
"            <link name=\"link\">\n"
"                <collision name=\"collision\">\n"
"                <geometry>\n"
"                    <plane>\n"
"                    <normal>0 0 1</normal>\n"
"                    </plane>\n"
"                </geometry>\n"
"                </collision>\n"
"                <visual name=\"visual\">\n"
"                <geometry>\n"
"                    <plane>\n"
"                    <normal>0 0 1</normal>\n"
"                    <size>100 100</size>\n"
"                    </plane>\n"
"                </geometry>\n"
"                <material>\n"
"                    <ambient>0.8 0.8 0.8 1</ambient>\n"
"                    <diffuse>0.8 0.8 0.8 1</diffuse>\n"
"                    <specular>0.8 0.8 0.8 1</specular>\n"
"                </material>\n"
"                </visual>\n"
"            </link>\n"
"        </model>\n"
"    </world>\n"
"</sdf>\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:79
msgid ""
"Save the file, navigate to the directory where you saved the file and launch "
"the simulator:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:81 ../../../building_robot.md:178
#: ../../../building_robot.md:435
msgid "`gz sim building_robot.sdf`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:83
msgid ""
"**Note**: You can name your file any name and save it anywhere on your "
"computer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:85
msgid ""
"You should see an empty world with just a ground plane and a sun light. "
"Check [World demo](sdf_worlds) to learn how to build your own world."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:87
msgid "Building a model"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:89
msgid "Under the `</model>` tag we will add our robot model as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:91
msgid "Defining the model"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:93
msgid ""
"<model name='vehicle_blue' canonical_link='chassis'>\n"
"    <pose relative_to='world'>0 0 0 0 0 0</pose>\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:98
msgid ""
"Here we define the name of our model `vehicle_blue`, which should be a "
"unique name among its siblings (other tags or models on the same level). "
"Each model may have one link designated as the `canonical_link`, the "
"implicit frame of the model is attached to this link. If not defined, the "
"first `<link>` will be chosen as the canonical link. The `<pose>` tag is "
"used to define the position and orientation of our model and the "
"`relative_to` attribute is used to define the pose of the model relative to "
"any other frame. If `relative_to` is not defined, the model's `<pose>` will "
"be relative to the world."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:103
msgid ""
"Let's make our pose relative to the `world`. The values inside the pose tag "
"are as follows: `<pose>X Y Z R P Y</pose>`, where the `X Y Z` represent the "
"position of the frame and `R P Y` represent the orientation in roll pitch "
"yaw. We set them to zeros which makes the two frames (the model and the "
"world) identical."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:106
msgid "Links forming our robot"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:108
msgid ""
"Every model is a group of `links` (can be just one link) connected together "
"with `joints`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:110
msgid "Chassis"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:112
msgid ""
"    <link name='chassis'>\n"
"        <pose relative_to='__model__'>0.5 0 0.4 0 0 0</pose>\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:117
msgid ""
"We define the first link, the `chassis` of our car and it's pose relative to "
"the `model`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:119
msgid "Inertial properties"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:121
msgid ""
"    <inertial> <!--inertial properties of the link mass, inertia matrix-->\n"
"        <mass>1.14395</mass>\n"
"        <inertia>\n"
"            <ixx>0.095329</ixx>\n"
"            <ixy>0</ixy>\n"
"            <ixz>0</ixz>\n"
"            <iyy>0.381317</iyy>\n"
"            <iyz>0</iyz>\n"
"            <izz>0.476646</izz>\n"
"        </inertia>\n"
"    </inertial>\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:135
msgid ""
"Here we define the inertial properties of the chassis like the `<mass>` and "
"the `<inertia>` matrix. The values of the inertia matrix for primitive "
"shapes can be calculated using this [tool](https://amesweb.info/inertia/mass-"
"moment-of-inertia-calculator.aspx)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:137
msgid "Visual and collision"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:139
msgid ""
"    <visual name='visual'>\n"
"        <geometry>\n"
"            <box>\n"
"                <size>2.0 1.0 0.5</size>\n"
"            </box>\n"
"        </geometry>\n"
"        <!--let's add color to our link-->\n"
"        <material>\n"
"            <ambient>0.0 0.0 1.0 1</ambient>\n"
"            <diffuse>0.0 0.0 1.0 1</diffuse>\n"
"            <specular>0.0 0.0 1.0 1</specular>\n"
"        </material>\n"
"    </visual>\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:155
msgid ""
"As the name suggests, the `<visual>` tag is responsible for how our link "
"will look. We define the shape of our link inside the `<geometry>` tag as a "
"`<box>` (cuboid) and then specify the three dimensions (in meters) of this "
"box inside the `<size>` tag. Then, inside the `<material>` tag we define the "
"material of our link. Here we defined the `<ambient>`, `<diffuse>` and "
"`<specular>` colors in a set of four numbers red/green/blue/alpha each in "
"range [0, 1]."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:160
msgid ""
"        <collision name='collision'>\n"
"            <geometry>\n"
"                <box>\n"
"                    <size>2.0 1.0 0.5</size>\n"
"                </box>\n"
"            </geometry>\n"
"        </collision>\n"
"    </link>\n"
"</model>\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:172
msgid ""
"The `<collision>` tag defines the collision properties of the link, how our "
"link will react with other objects and the effect of the physics engine on "
"it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:174
msgid ""
"**Note**: `<collision>` can be different from the visual properties, for "
"example, simpler collision models are often used to reduce computation time."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:176
msgid ""
"After copying all the parts above into the world file in order, run the "
"world again:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:180
msgid "Our model should look like this:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:182
msgid "![car chassis](tutorials/building_robot/chassis.png)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:182
msgid "car chassis"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:184
msgid ""
"In the top left toolbar, click the Translate icon, then select your model. "
"You should see three axes like this:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:187
msgid "![model_axis](tutorials/building_robot/model_axis.png)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:187
msgid "model_axis"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:189
msgid ""
"These are the axes of our model where red is the x-axis, green is the y-axis "
"and blue is the z-axis."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:191
msgid "Left wheel"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:193
msgid ""
"Let's add wheels to our robot. The following code goes after the `</link>` "
"tag and before the `</model>` tag. All the links and joints belonging to the "
"same model should be defined before the `</model>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:195
msgid ""
"<link name='left_wheel'>\n"
"    <pose relative_to=\"chassis\">-0.5 0.6 0 -1.5707 0 0</pose>\n"
"    <inertial>\n"
"        <mass>1</mass>\n"
"        <inertia>\n"
"            <ixx>0.043333</ixx>\n"
"            <ixy>0</ixy>\n"
"            <ixz>0</ixz>\n"
"            <iyy>0.043333</iyy>\n"
"            <iyz>0</iyz>\n"
"            <izz>0.08</izz>\n"
"        </inertia>\n"
"    </inertial>\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:211
msgid ""
"We defined the name of our link `left_wheel` and then defined its `<pose>` "
"`relative_to` the `chassis` link. The wheel needed to be placed on the left "
"to the back of the `chassis` so that's why we chose the values for `pose` as "
"`-0.5 0.6 0`. Also, our wheel is a cylinder, but on its side. That's why we "
"defined the orientation value as `-1.5707 0 0` which is a `-90` degree "
"rotation around the x-axis (the angles are in radians). Then we defined the "
"`inertial` properties of the wheel, the `mass` and the `inertia` matrix."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:217
msgid "Visualization and Collision"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:219
msgid ""
"    <visual name='visual'>\n"
"        <geometry>\n"
"            <cylinder>\n"
"                <radius>0.4</radius>\n"
"                <length>0.2</length>\n"
"            </cylinder>\n"
"        </geometry>\n"
"        <material>\n"
"            <ambient>1.0 0.0 0.0 1</ambient>\n"
"            <diffuse>1.0 0.0 0.0 1</diffuse>\n"
"            <specular>1.0 0.0 0.0 1</specular>\n"
"        </material>\n"
"    </visual>\n"
"    <collision name='collision'>\n"
"        <geometry>\n"
"            <cylinder>\n"
"                <radius>0.4</radius>\n"
"                <length>0.2</length>\n"
"            </cylinder>\n"
"        </geometry>\n"
"    </collision>\n"
"</link>\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:244
msgid ""
"The `<visual>` and  the `<collision>` properties are similar to the previous "
"link, except the shape of our link has the shape of `<cylinder>` that "
"requires two attributes: the `<radius>` and the `<length>` of the cylinder. "
"Save the file and run the world again, our model should look like this:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:247
msgid "![this](tutorials/building_robot/car_left_wheel.png)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:247
msgid "this"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:249
msgid "Right wheel"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:251
msgid ""
"<!--The same as left wheel but with different position-->\n"
"<link name='right_wheel'>\n"
"    <pose relative_to=\"chassis\">-0.5 -0.6 0 -1.5707 0 0</pose> <!--angles "
"are in radian-->\n"
"    <inertial>\n"
"        <mass>1</mass>\n"
"        <inertia>\n"
"            <ixx>0.043333</ixx>\n"
"            <ixy>0</ixy>\n"
"            <ixz>0</ixz>\n"
"            <iyy>0.043333</iyy>\n"
"            <iyz>0</iyz>\n"
"            <izz>0.08</izz>\n"
"        </inertia>\n"
"    </inertial>\n"
"    <visual name='visual'>\n"
"        <geometry>\n"
"            <cylinder>\n"
"                <radius>0.4</radius>\n"
"                <length>0.2</length>\n"
"            </cylinder>\n"
"        </geometry>\n"
"        <material>\n"
"            <ambient>1.0 0.0 0.0 1</ambient>\n"
"            <diffuse>1.0 0.0 0.0 1</diffuse>\n"
"            <specular>1.0 0.0 0.0 1</specular>\n"
"        </material>\n"
"    </visual>\n"
"    <collision name='collision'>\n"
"        <geometry>\n"
"            <cylinder>\n"
"                <radius>0.4</radius>\n"
"                <length>0.2</length>\n"
"            </cylinder>\n"
"        </geometry>\n"
"    </collision>\n"
"</link>\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:290
msgid "The right wheel is similar to the left wheel except for its position."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:292
msgid "Defining an arbitrary frame"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:294
msgid ""
"As of SDF 1.7 (Fortress uses SDF 1.8), we can define arbitrary frames. It "
"takes two attributes:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:296
msgid "`name`: the name of the frame"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:297
msgid ""
"`attached_to`: the name of the frame or the link to which this frame is "
"attached."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:299
msgid "Let's add a frame for our caster wheel as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:301
msgid ""
"<frame name=\"caster_frame\" attached_to='chassis'>\n"
"    <pose>0.8 0 -0.2 0 0 0</pose>\n"
"</frame>\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:307
msgid ""
"We gave our frame name `caster_frame` and attached it to the `chassis` link, "
"then the `<pose>` tag to define the position and orientation of the frame. "
"We didn't use the `relative_to` attribute so the pose is with respect to the "
"frame named in the `attached_to` attribute, `chassis` in our case."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:310
msgid "Caster wheel"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:312
msgid ""
"<!--caster wheel-->\n"
"<link name='caster'>\n"
"    <pose relative_to='caster_frame'/>\n"
"    <inertial>\n"
"        <mass>1</mass>\n"
"        <inertia>\n"
"            <ixx>0.016</ixx>\n"
"            <ixy>0</ixy>\n"
"            <ixz>0</ixz>\n"
"            <iyy>0.016</iyy>\n"
"            <iyz>0</iyz>\n"
"            <izz>0.016</izz>\n"
"        </inertia>\n"
"    </inertial>\n"
"    <visual name='visual'>\n"
"        <geometry>\n"
"            <sphere>\n"
"                <radius>0.2</radius>\n"
"            </sphere>\n"
"        </geometry>\n"
"        <material>\n"
"            <ambient>0.0 1 0.0 1</ambient>\n"
"            <diffuse>0.0 1 0.0 1</diffuse>\n"
"            <specular>0.0 1 0.0 1</specular>\n"
"        </material>\n"
"    </visual>\n"
"    <collision name='collision'>\n"
"        <geometry>\n"
"            <sphere>\n"
"                <radius>0.2</radius>\n"
"            </sphere>\n"
"        </geometry>\n"
"    </collision>\n"
"</link>\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:349
msgid ""
"Our last link is the `caster` and its pose is with respect to the frame "
"`caster_frame` we defined above. As you could notice we closed the `pose` "
"tag without defining the position or the orientation; in this case the pose "
"of the link is the same as (identity) the frame in `relative_to`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:352
msgid ""
"In the `<visual>` and `<collision>` tags we defined a different shape "
"`<sphere>` which requires the `<radius>` of the sphere."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:354
msgid "Connecting links together (joints)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:356
msgid ""
"We need to connect these links together; here comes the job of the `<joint>` "
"tag. The joint tag connects two links together and defines how they will "
"move with respect to each other. Inside the `<joint>` tag we need to define "
"the two links to connect and their relations (way of movement)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:360
msgid "Left wheel joint"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:362
msgid ""
"<joint name='left_wheel_joint' type='revolute'>\n"
"    <pose relative_to='left_wheel'/>\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:367
msgid ""
"Our first joint is the `left_wheel_joint`. It takes two attributes: the name "
"`name='left_wheel_joint'` and the type `type='revolute'`. the `revolute` "
"type gives 1 rotational degree of freedom with joint limits. The pose of the "
"joint is the same as the child link frame, which is the `left_wheel` frame."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:372
msgid ""
"    <parent>chassis</parent>\n"
"    <child>left_wheel</child>\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:377
msgid ""
"Every joint connects two links (bodies) together. Here we connect the "
"`chassis` with the `left_wheel`. `chassis` is the parent link and "
"`left_wheel` is the child link."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:381
msgid ""
"    <axis>\n"
"        <xyz expressed_in='__model__'>0 1 0</xyz> <!--can be defined as any "
"frame or even arbitrary frames-->\n"
"        <limit>\n"
"            <lower>-1.79769e+308</lower>    <!--negative infinity-->\n"
"            <upper>1.79769e+308</upper>     <!--positive infinity-->\n"
"        </limit>\n"
"    </axis>\n"
"</joint>\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:392
msgid ""
"Here we define the axis of rotation. The axis of rotation can be any frame, "
"not just the `parent` or the `child` link. We chose the y-axis with respect "
"to the `model` frame so we put `1` in the y element and zeros in the others. "
"For the revolute joint we need to define the `<limits>` of our rotation "
"angle in the `<lower>` and `<upper>` tags."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:397
msgid "**Note**: The angles are in radians."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:399
msgid "Right wheel joint"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:401
msgid ""
"The `right_wheel_joint` is very similar except for the pose of the joint. "
"This joint connects the `right_wheel` with the `chassis`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:404
msgid ""
"<joint name='right_wheel_joint' type='revolute'>\n"
"    <pose relative_to='right_wheel'/>\n"
"    <parent>chassis</parent>\n"
"    <child>right_wheel</child>\n"
"    <axis>\n"
"        <xyz expressed_in='__model__'>0 1 0</xyz>\n"
"        <limit>\n"
"            <lower>-1.79769e+308</lower>    <!--negative infinity-->\n"
"            <upper>1.79769e+308</upper>     <!--positive infinity-->\n"
"        </limit>\n"
"    </axis>\n"
"</joint>\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:419
msgid "Caster wheel joint"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:421
msgid ""
"For the caster we need a different type of joint (connection). We used "
"`type='ball'` which gives 3 rotational degrees of freedom."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../building_robot.md:424
msgid ""
"<joint name='caster_wheel' type='ball'>\n"
"    <parent>chassis</parent>\n"
"    <child>caster</child>\n"
"</joint>\n"
msgstr ""

